__d(function(g,r,i,a,_m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(e,"LEARN_LESSONS",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return t}}),e.learnLessonById=function(s){return s?t.find(t=>t.id===s):void 0},e.formatLessonDuration=function(t){const s=Math.floor(t/60),o=Math.round(t%60);return s>0?`${s} min ${o}s`:`${o}s`};const t=[{id:'compression-vs-rebound',title:'Compression vs rebound',kicker:'Shocks',summary:'How the damper controls speed, and why preload is not spring rate.',keyLine:'The spring holds the weight. The damper controls the speed.',durationSec:85,videoFile:'compression-vs-rebound.mp4',posterFile:'compression-vs-rebound-poster.jpg',captionsFile:'compression-vs-rebound.vtt',sections:[{id:'compression',title:'Compression',body:'A bump pushes the shaft into the shock body and the spring takes the load. Oil is forced through the piston and bends the compression shims open. That resistance controls how fast the suspension compresses. Too much feels harsh, and every bump kicks through the chassis. Too little feels soft and floaty: it wallows, and bottoms out on big hits.'},{id:'rebound',title:'Rebound',body:'The spring then pushes the shaft back out. Oil flows the other way through the rebound shims, which sets how fast the wheel returns. Too much rebound and the wheel cannot come back in time, so it packs down over repeated bumps. Too little and it is bouncy, and pogos after every bump.'},{id:'preload',title:'Preload',body:'Preload is how far the spring is already squeezed when it is installed. You set it by winding the collar on the shock body. More preload lifts ride height and reduces sag, but it does not change the spring rate \u2014 the spring is no stiffer. If you need a lot of preload to get the right sag, the spring is probably too soft. For side-by-sides, the Weller Racing guide says more than about 51 mm of total preload usually means the springs are too soft.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'Adjust & save',href:'/tune/sxs-adjust'}]},{id:'spring-rate',title:'Spring rate and preload',kicker:'Springs',summary:'How stiff the spring is, how that reaches the wheel, and why preload is not a rate change.',keyLine:'Spring rate is the stiffness. Preload only sets ride height, not the rate.',durationSec:135,videoFile:'spring-rate.mp4',posterFile:'spring-rate-poster.jpg',captionsFile:'spring-rate.vtt',sections:[{id:'rate',title:'Spring rate',body:'Spring rate is how much force it takes to squash the spring by one millimetre. It is measured in newtons per millimetre, or kilograms per millimetre \u2014 one kilogram per millimetre is about 9.81 N/mm. On a linear spring, double the load and it compresses twice as far. Too soft and the vehicle wallows, rolls and bottoms out. Too stiff and it feels harsh, and the tyre can skip over bumps and lose grip.'},{id:'wheel-rate',title:'Wheel rate',body:'The tyre does not feel the spring rate directly. It feels the wheel rate: roughly the spring rate, times the motion ratio squared, times a correction for how far the shock leans. A spring that looks stiff on the bench can be quite soft at the wheel if the shock sits inboard or at a lean.'},{id:'preload',title:'Preload',body:'Preload is how far the spring is already squeezed when it is installed. You set it by winding the collar on the shock body. More preload lifts ride height and reduces sag, but it does not make the spring stiffer. If you need a lot of preload to get the right sag, the spring is probably too soft. For side-by-sides, the Weller Racing guide says more than about 51 mm of total preload usually means the springs are too soft.'},{id:'types',title:'Linear, dual-rate and progressive',body:'A linear spring has one rate all the way through. A dual-rate setup stacks a softer tender spring on the main spring; the tender closes first, then the rate steps up at the crossover. A progressive spring gets gradually stiffer as it compresses. Pick the rate for the weight, then use sag to check it.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'Adjust & save',href:'/tune/sxs-adjust'},{label:'Handling Wizard',href:'/tune/handling-wizard'}]},{id:'ride-height-and-sag',title:'Ride height and sag',kicker:'Setup',summary:'How much the suspension settles under the vehicle\u2019s weight, and how to set it before you chase clickers.',keyLine:'Sag is how much the suspension settles under the vehicle\u2019s weight. Set that first.',sections:[{id:'what',title:'What sag is',body:'Sag is the amount of travel used when the vehicle is sitting still at its normal ready-to-drive weight. Ride height is the result: where the chassis sits relative to the axles or the ground. Get this in the right window before you chase damping, because clickers cannot fix a spring that is holding the vehicle too high or too low.'},{id:'measure',title:'How to measure it',body:'On level ground, pick a repeatable point such as the shock body or a fender lip, and measure to a mark on the axle or upright. First support the vehicle so the suspension is at full droop, then again at rest with fuel, a driver and your usual gear. The difference is sag. You can also write it as a percentage of total wheel travel. Many off-road vehicles sit around a quarter to a third of their travel as sag, but follow the spring or shock maker for your machine.'},{id:'preload',title:'Using preload',body:'Use the spring collar for small ride-height changes. If you need a lot of preload to reach the sag you want, or the collar is nearly backed off and it is still too high, the spring rate is probably wrong \u2014 change the spring rather than winding the collar to its limit.'},{id:'rake',title:'Front and rear',body:'Set both ends, then check the rake: how the rear ride height sits relative to the front. A small nose-down or level stance is typical, depending on the vehicle. Changing one end moves weight and can change how it steers, so re-check both after an adjustment.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'Handling Wizard',href:'/tune/handling-wizard'},{label:'Adjust & save',href:'/tune/sxs-adjust'}]},{id:'bump-stops-and-nitrogen',title:'Bump stops and nitrogen pressure',kicker:'Shocks',summary:'How the bump stop catches the last of the travel, and what nitrogen pressure actually changes.',keyLine:'The bump stop catches the last of the travel so the shock does not bottom out hard.',sections:[{id:'bump-stops',title:'Bump stops',body:'A bump stop \u2014 foam, rubber or a hydraulic bottom-out \u2014 should start working near the end of the travel and ramp up so the shock never slams metal to metal. If it engages too early, the vehicle feels harsh on moderate hits. If it engages too late, or is too soft, big landings crash through and can damage the shock.'},{id:'nitrogen',title:'Nitrogen pressure',body:'Nitrogen in a charged shock helps the piston stay in control and reduces oil cavitation. Raising the pressure can also add a little support and slightly lift ride height, because the gas acts like a weak spring. It is not a substitute for the right spring rate. Always stay inside the shock maker\u2019s pressure range, and check pressures cold.'},{id:'tool',title:'In the app',body:'The bump stop tool helps you think through shock and bump-stop nitrogen together, using the travel and weight you already have on the vehicle. Use it to see whether a change in pressure or stop length is doing the work, then confirm it on the vehicle.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'Bump stops',href:'/tune/bump-stops'}]},{id:'sway-bars-and-roll-split',title:'Sway bars and roll split',kicker:'Handling',summary:'How a sway bar resists body roll, and how front versus rear stiffness changes the way it steers.',keyLine:'The sway bar resists body roll. Front versus rear stiffness is the roll split.',sections:[{id:'what',title:'What a sway bar does',body:'A sway bar (anti-roll bar) ties the left and right sides of an axle or independent suspension together with a torsion bar. When the body leans, it resists that lean. It does little when both wheels go up together, so it is for roll, not for keeping the ride soft in a straight line.'},{id:'split',title:'Roll split',body:'Roll split is how much of that roll resistance comes from the front bar versus the rear. As a starting point, a stiffer front bar (or a softer rear) tends to make the vehicle push more, or understeer. A stiffer rear bar (or a softer front) tends to make the rear step wide, or oversteer. Change one end at a time and test the same corner.'},{id:'disconnect',title:'Disconnecting',body:'On a 4x4, a disconnect or a quick-release lets the axle articulate more in the rocks, then you reconnect it for the road or dunes. A bar that is always connected will limit droop on one wheel. The sway bar tool works out bar rate and roll split from the bar you have, and when a disconnect is useful.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'Sway bars',href:'/tune/sway-bars'},{label:'Handling Wizard',href:'/tune/handling-wizard'}]},{id:'four-link-basics',title:'4-link basics',kicker:'Geometry',summary:'Anti-squat, anti-dive, roll axis and instant centre, in plain language.',keyLine:'The links set anti-squat, anti-dive, the roll axis and the instant centre.',sections:[{id:'instant-centre',title:'Instant centre',body:'Seen from the side, if you extend the upper and lower links until they meet, that meeting point is the instant centre. Its height and how far it sits ahead of or behind the axle set how the axle wants to move under power, braking and roll.'},{id:'anti-squat',title:'Anti-squat',body:'Anti-squat is how much the rear links resist the body squatting under power. 100% means no squat at all. Too little and the rear squats and the springs do all the work. Too much and the rear can jack up, hook or feel tight over ripples. The 4-link Wizard rates this against the build profile you pick.'},{id:'anti-dive',title:'Anti-dive',body:'Anti-dive is the front-end version under braking: how much the links resist the nose diving. The same geometry that helps one can hurt the other, which is why you measure both ends rather than copying a rear link layout onto the front.'},{id:'roll-axis',title:'Roll axis and roll centre',body:'Each axle has a roll centre, the point that end of the body rolls about. The line joining the front and rear roll centres is the roll axis. Its height and tilt change how much the body rolls and how the axles steer as it leans. The wizard\u2019s live readout updates anti-squat, anti-dive, roll axis and instant centre as you enter the link points.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'4-link Wizard',href:'/tune/four-link'}]},{id:'sxs-clicker-order',title:'Clicker tuning order for SxS',kicker:'SxS',summary:'A simple order for side-by-side clickers: springs and sag first, then one adjuster family at a time.',keyLine:'Get the spring and sag right first, then change one clicker family at a time.',sections:[{id:'first',title:'Start with the spring',body:'Clickers cannot fix the wrong spring. Set spring rate and sag (and therefore ride height) first, and only then touch the adjusters. If the vehicle sits too low or too high, or needs a lot of preload, change the spring before you chase damping.'},{id:'order',title:'The order',body:'Work from the rebound inward. First set rebound so the wheel returns in time without packing down or pogoing. Then low-speed compression, which controls slower body motion: squat, dive and rolling through a corner. Then high-speed compression, which is for sharp hits and harshness. Change one family at a time, one or two clicks, and test the same loop before you move on.'},{id:'notes',title:'Keep notes',body:'Write down the stock clicks and your changes. If a change makes it worse, go back one step rather than stacking more clicks on top. Adjust & save can store a setup next to stock for the same machine, and the Handling Wizard can point you at the next change if you describe how it feels.'}],relatedTools:[{label:'Adjust & save',href:'/tune/sxs-adjust'},{label:'Handling Wizard',href:'/tune/handling-wizard'}]}]},847,[]);
